<h1>About</h1>

<section class="content-section">
<h2>How I operate</h2>
<p>
I'm wired as an entrepreneur, not a consultant. That means I'd rather notice something's wrong and just fix it than write you a brief about it. I spotted Freddy's flyer had the wrong website address on it simply by staying in regular contact with him — not from a briefing document — and it was fixed inside the hour.
</p>
<p>
Think of it less like hiring an outside contractor and more like picking up a co-founder for a few hours a week — someone who's actually built and sold a company, gets into the actual detail, and moves at the speed a founder moves at, not the speed an agency moves at.
</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
<h2>Why one generalist, not five specialists</h2>
<p>
Most small businesses start the same way: a skilled technician — a gardener, a plumber, a photographer — has what the E-Myth calls an "entrepreneurial spasm" and decides to go it alone. They're good at the trade. Nobody taught them the website, the marketing, the systems — so it either doesn't get done, or they end up doing it themselves, badly, on top of the actual work.
</p>
<p>
The old fix was hiring it out piecemeal: a web designer for the site, a marketing person for the ads, an IT contractor for the systems. Each one is good at their one thing — and each one only ever sees their one thing. Most small businesses can't afford that many specialists anyway.
</p>
<p>
AI changes what's actually needed. A human specialist isn't going to out-build what AI can now do inside a narrow skill, so the value of the niche expert is shrinking, and the value of the generalist who actually understands the founder and the business is growing. I don't wait to be told there's a problem with your CRM, or your socials, or your flyer, or your website — because having the context to see where they connect is the job. That's what happened with Freddy: the website, the flyer, the CRM, the Google listing, a marketing video, all touched by the same person, because they were never really separate problems.
</p>
<p>
I'm not doing all of it by hand, either — I delegate the actual execution to specialist AI tools, whatever the job needs. What I hold onto is the context: what this business actually needs right now, which keeps changing as it grows, faster than you could keep re-explaining it to a new specialist every time one's called in.
</p>
<p>
That's the holistic bit: not five vendors who each own a slice, and not you doing it all alone once the spasm wears off — but one person who actually knows your business, doing the work across all of it.
</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
<h2>The loop</h2>
<p>
It isn't a one-off project. It's a loop: get involved, talk, understand the business, diagnose what's actually slowing it down, and deliver a quick win — something real and fast, not a six-week plan. That quick win changes how the business runs, which means the next constraint is now something different to what it was before.
</p>
<p>
Say the problem was never enough leads. Fix that, and there's a new problem: too many enquiries coming in for one person to keep on top of. So the next fix is getting that organised properly — which might surface the one after that: not enough people, or not senior enough people, to handle what's now coming in. Each fix changes the machine, which throws up the next thing. That's where I stay useful — not because I did one clever thing once, but because I'm still there for what it turns into.
</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
<h2>Who this is for</h2>
<p>
I'm not interested in selling vitamins — nice-to-haves for a business that's happy staying the size it is. I'm here for the paracetamol cases: entrepreneurs who are actually trying to grow, who've got something getting in the way of that, and need it dealt with now.
</p>
<p>
If you want to run one shop, one van, one practice, forever, and just want it a bit cheaper or a bit tidier — that's a perfectly good way to run a business. It's just not what I'm built for. I'm for people building something bigger than themselves, who don't want to still be the bottleneck in it two years from now.
</p>
</section>

